comments[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-11T05:25:45.117Z","harness:claude-code","Measurement and implementation map, taken on the live 2429-item tracker at 2026-08-11.\n\nMeasured today\n- pm stats default TOON 745 bytes, --json 957 bytes. The row_contract block is 285 bytes, 38 percent of the default.\n- by_type emits 17 rows, 7 of them zero: Card, Changeset, Event, Meeting, TwinAsset, TwinFacility, VcsRef.\n- by_status emits 6 rows, 3 of them zero: draft, in_progress, blocked.\n- Non-zero types are Issue 820, Task 660, Feature 423, Chore 289, Epic 89, Decision 64, Story 53, Plan 23, Milestone 7, Reminder 1.\n- A reshaped default carrying the same totals plus a per-type lifecycle split, minus zero rows and minus the row contract, renders at 468 bytes: 37 percent smaller and strictly more informative.\n\nWhere the zero rows come from\nNot from workspace configuration. settings item_types definitions is empty and the workspace schema block is empty. pm schema list reports 17 types as 11 builtin, 1 custom, 5 extension. Event and Meeting are builtin and unused here. Story is the one custom type and is in active use with 53 items, so a rule that hides non-builtin types would hide a type this project uses - the correct rule is to hide empty buckets, not foreign ones. Card comes from the bundled kanban-profile extension, TwinAsset and TwinFacility from digital-twin-exemplar, Changeset and VcsRef from vcs-exemplar; all three are bundled builtin extension directories that load without being installed.\n\nImplementation map\n- The zero-fill originates in zeroByType and zeroByStatus in the stats module, which pre-seed a bucket for every registry entry before counting.\n- The lifecycle split needs no new logic: groupItemsByDimension in the metadata-coverage module already accepts a type dimension and returns lifecycle-bucketed rows. It is the same primitive that serves --by-assignee, --by-tag and --by-priority, and stats already builds the classifier it needs.\n- The row contract for stats is declared in the read-output projection table as row_keys by_type and by_status. Changing the shape of by_type without updating that declaration would leave the published jq selector pointing at a shape that no longer exists.\n- The zero-fill is documented as deliberate in docs/COMMANDS.md and that sentence has to go in the same change.\n- The stats default is absent from the token-budget-gate corpus, which currently carries an activity-default entry but no stats entry. Adding one is what turns the saving into an enforced ceiling.\n\nCross-check\npm aggregate --group-by type --completion already answers the per-type lifecycle question, at roughly 1.5 kB and as a second invocation. Folding it into the stats default removes one round trip from every orientation sequence rather than adding a surface.\n"
files[6]{path,scope,note}:
  docs/COMMANDS.md,project,documents the zero-fill as intentional; that claim is retired by this change
  scripts/release/token-budget-gate.mjs,project,corpus has no stats entry; adding one turns the saving into an enforced ceiling
  src/cli/register-operations.ts,project,stats command registration and the restore flag
  src/core/governance/metadata-coverage.ts,project,groupItemsByDimension already supports the type dimension and returns lifecycle buckets
  src/sdk/output-projection.ts,project,stats row contract declares row_keys by_type and by_status
  src/sdk/stats.ts,project,zeroByType/zeroByStatus pre-seed the empty buckets; assembleStatsResult owns the default shape
